A||||13||Matthew_Fiero||hydraulics replacement||||1||
Z||000000||Matthew_Fiero||10-08-2001||11:00 PM||||to make a long stupid story short, I poured 5w30 into my clutch master cylinder<BR>so i need to replace all the hydraulics<BR>what is the best way to go to replace it?<BR>would i need, a new master and slave, the line that goes to the slave (that is probably bent) and a hydraulic line?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2001||12:34 AM||||Just a master and slave. The line can be flushed out. Check in theogres cave and the archives, you should find a procedure to use.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Matthew_Fiero||10-09-2001||07:56 AM||||couldn't find anything in orges cave...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Matthew_Fiero||10-09-2001||02:13 PM||||Not one person knows?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2001||04:13 PM||||You can use alcohol to flush your lines out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||10-09-2001||07:18 PM||||Get a vacuum pump and vacuum the reservoir dry. Fill with clean fluid, go to the other end and start vacuuming till you get clean fluid. You could also let the system gravity drain for a while before you use the vacuum.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-09-2001||08:28 PM||||DO NOT do as jake said. That, or anything like it, will force oil down the entire system.<P>IF you haven't used it, you may only need a new master cylinder and a "Back Flush" of the hard line. The oil doesn't instantly spread but it won't take long on a system in use.<P>If you've spread the oil down the hardline by using it or worse, flushing, then the MC, slave, and flex line must all be replaced.<P>To back flush the line...<P>Disconnect the MC. put a secion of plastic tubing on the steel line and run it to a bucket.<P>disconnect the softline(rubber) from the hardline(steel) and force 90% or stronger isopropyl alcohol up toward the MC. You can do that with a big syringe. A turkey baster would even work. you need volume more than pressure.<P>after flushing, blast the line out with CLEAN DRY OIL FREE air. The air MUST NOT pass thru tool oilers or any air lines that have ever been used with oiled tool air. Do Not use "canned" air. Many "canned air"  products are actually petrolium based.<P>The MC has to go. It's unlikely the thing is oil free. It will likely fail soon. You might get lucky and be able to save the slave and flexline. It's worth a try. Either way you've got to flush the hardline before new parts are installed.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
